# Create doc

When the user applies **create-doc** to a markdown file under `Personal/Files/azure/`, rewrite that file in place so it matches `Personal/Files/azure/sample.md`. Read `sample.md` once and follow its section order, accordion markup, and spacing. Do not rewrite `sample.md`. Do not append a second copy of the note.

Only run this workflow when the user asks to apply the rule. Ordinary edits do not reformat the file.

## Page order

1. Keep the existing back link as the first line. Do not change its target.
2. `# Chapter N — Title` with an em dash. Keep the chapter number and the subject of the original `#` heading.
3. `## Table of Contents` linking Introduction, Detailed tutorial, each accordion, Summary, and References. Nest accordion links under Detailed tutorial.
4. `## Introduction` — a few sentences on what the chapter teaches and why it matters. Not a copy of the first heading.
5. `## Detailed tutorial` — one bold source line, then one `<details>` accordion per idea.
6. `## Summary` — short bullets of the facts a reader should remember. Not a restatement of every heading.
7. `## References` — links already in the file, then official Microsoft Learn pages for topics the chapter names. Do not invent URLs.

## Accordions

One accordion is one idea. Merge a unit's introduction, repeated learning objectives, and closing summary into the section they belong to. Drop LMS chrome: Completed, XP, minutes, and "Next unit".

```markdown
<details id="what-cloud-computing-is">
<summary><strong>What cloud computing is</strong></summary>

Cloud computing is the delivery of computing services over the internet.

![Seasonal retail launch](https://example.invalid/image.png)

_Figure: Extra capacity is added for a launch, then released when traffic falls._

</details>
```

- `id` is the kebab-case slug used in the table of contents.
- `<summary>` is sentence case, wrapped in `<strong>`, and names the idea.
- One blank line after `</summary>` and one blank line before `</details>`.
- Keep definitions, examples, numbers, exam weights, and quiz answers.
- Turn tab-aligned comparisons into Markdown tables.
- Keep every image. Give it meaningful alt text and an italic `*Figure: ...*` caption that says what the picture shows.
- Put each module's knowledge check in its own accordion. State the correct option in words.

## Voice

Write the way the sample is written: short paragraphs, plain sentences, tables when a comparison is the point. Keep Microsoft's exam terms (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS, CapEx, OpEx, SLA). Do not add greetings, and do not leave the raw dump's navigation lines in the body.
